2500 Tree Diagram Ruleset
=========================

Illustrates tree-like architecture or workflow diagram in plain text.


Structure
---------

1. Use [ ] for major components, services, resources, or logical containers.

   ├─ [ Component A ]
   ├─ [ Component B ]
   └─ [ Component C ]

2. Use ├─, └─, and │ to nest child components under parent components
   for hierarchy, ownership, or containment.

   [ Parent Component ]
    ├─ [ Child Component A ]
    └─ [ Child Component B ]

   Nesting already shows basic parent-child relationships.

3. Use < > for placeholders, omitted items, repeated patterns, or variable names.

   ├─ Name: < component name >
   ├─ Type: <>
   └─ < other components >

4. Do not bracket small details.

   Brackets are only for major components.

   Good:
   ├─ [ Component A ]
   │   └─ Color: blue

   Avoid:
   ├─ [ Component A ]
   │   └─ [ Color: blue ]


Content
-------

5. Use key-value lines for attributes.

   ├─ Key: value

   Examples:
   ├─ Name: item-one
   ├─ Size: medium
   ├─ Type: <>
   └─ Role: <>

6. Use numbered action lines when sequence matters.

   ├─ (1) Action made: optional summary
   ├─ (2) Action made: optional summary
   ├─ (3a) Action made: optional summary
   └─ (3b) Action made: optional summary

   Repeat a number with a letter suffix for simultaneous actions.

7. Place actions under the closest component where the action was made.

   Actions belong to the component that performs or owns them.

   Good:
   ├─ [ Sender ]
   │   └─ (1) Sends a request
   │
   └─ [ Receiver ]

   Avoid:
   ├─ [ Sender ]
   │
   ├─ (1) Sends a request
   │
   └─ [ Receiver ]

8. Use bullet lines for action details.

   Place details under the related action.

   ├─ (1) Action made: optional summary
   │     * action detail
   │     * action detail


Relationships
-------------

9. Use a Links section for relationships between components.

   Use Links when nesting is not enough to show the relationship.
   Place Links under the nearest common parent.

   ├─ Links:
   │  ├─ [ Source Component A ]
   │  │  --> [ Target Component A ]: relationship
   │  │
   │  └─ [ Source Component B ]
   │     <-> [ Target Component B ]: relationship

   Use --> for one-way relationships.
   Use <-> for two-way relationships.

10. Stack target components under common source component in Links.

   ├─ Links:
   │  └─ [ Source Component ]
   │     --> [ Target Component A ]: relationship
   │     <-> [ Target Component B ]: relationship


Style
-----

11. Keep one idea per line.

12. Prefer clarity over completeness.

13. Include only details that serve the diagram's purpose.


Template
--------

[ Root Component ]
 ├─ Name: optional-name
 ├─ < other attributes >
 │
 ├─ [ Parent Component A ]
 │   ├─ Role: optional-role
 │   ├─ < other attributes >
 │   │
 │   ├─ (1) Action made: optional summary
 │   │     * action detail
 │   │     * action detail
 │   │
 │   ├─ [ Child Component A ]
 │   │   └─ < other attributes or actions >
 │   │
 │   └─ [ Child Component B ]
 │       ├─ < other attributes >
 │       │
 │       └─ (2a) Action made: optional summary
 │
 ├─ [ Parent Component B ]
 │   ├─ Role: optional-role
 │   ├─ < other attributes >
 │   │
 │   └─ (2b) Action made: optional summary
 │
 ├─ Links:
 │  ├─ [ Child Component A ]
 │  │  --> [ Parent Component B ]: relationship
 │  │
 │  └─ [ Parent Component A ]
 │     --> [ Parent Component B ]: relationship                              
 │     <-> [ Root Component ]: relationship
 │
 └─ < other components >

Legend:
[ ] = component
(n) = action
--> = one-way relationship
<-> = two-way relationship
< > = placeholder
